{% doc %}
  The `<quantity-selector>` stepper: minus, a real number input, plus.

  ## Why this exists

  The same twenty lines were written out three times — in
  `blocks/product-quantity.liquid`, in `sections/quick-add.liquid` and again for
  the bundle picker's rows. Three copies of a control whose `aria-controls`,
  `aria-label`, `data-ref` names and `form` attribute all have to be exactly
  right for `assets/quantity-selector.js` to find them, and which the component's
  own module comment documents as one markup contract. The fourth copy is the
  one that would have got a `data-ref` wrong.

  So the markup lives here and the callers pass what actually differs: the id,
  the rules, and whether the label is visible.

  ## The input is real

  `name="quantity"`, `min`, `max` and `step` are on the element itself rather
  than only in the component's state, so add to cart works with JavaScript
  disabled and Shopify's own validation applies as a second line of defence.
  `form` lets the control sit outside the `<form>` it submits with, which is
  what the product page's sticky bar needs.

  ## Sizes

  `size` picks the stepper's dimensions from `assets/base.css` rather than
  passing pixels: `default` is the product page's, and `compact` is the pill in
  the bundle picker's rows — 24px round buttons in a dashed capsule, which is
  the size a control has to be when it sits on the same line as a rating.

  @param {string} id - The input's id. Must be unique on the page; `aria-controls` and the label both point at it.
  @param {number} [min] - Default 1.
  @param {number} [max] - Omitted means no maximum.
  @param {number} [step] - Default 1.
  @param {number} [value] - Starting value. Defaults to `min`.
  @param {string} [name] - The input's name. Default `quantity`.
  @param {string} [product_title] - Named in the buttons' accessible labels.
  @param {boolean} [show_label] - Render the label visibly. It is always rendered; this decides whether it is `visually-hidden`.
  @param {string} [form] - Id of the form this input submits with, when it sits outside it.
  @param {string} [size] - default | compact
  @param {string} [class] - Extra classes for the element.
  @param {string} [attributes] - Extra attributes for the element.
  @param {string} [rule] - The quantity-rule sentence, when there is one to show.
  @param {number} [icon_size] - Glyph size in px. Default 14, or 12 when compact.

  @example
    {% render 'quantity-selector',
       id: input_id,
       min: min,
       step: step,
       max: max,
       product_title: product.title,
       form: product_form_id %}
{% enddoc %}

{%- liquid
  assign qty_min = min | default: 1 | plus: 0
  assign qty_step = step | default: 1 | plus: 0
  assign qty_value = value | default: qty_min | plus: 0
  assign qty_name = name | default: 'quantity'

  assign variant_class = size | default: 'default'
  assign glyph = icon_size
  if glyph == nil
    if variant_class == 'compact'
      assign glyph = 12
    else
      assign glyph = 14
    endif
  endif

  assign label_class = 'quantity-selector__label'
  unless show_label
    assign label_class = 'visually-hidden'
  endunless

  comment
    The buttons say which product they belong to, because a bundle picker puts
    eight of these on one screen and "Increase quantity" eight times over tells
    a screen reader user nothing about which row they are on.

    With no title passed there is a second pair of strings rather than the first
    pair with an empty interpolation: "Decrease quantity for " with nothing
    after it is what a screen reader would read out, and a translator cannot fix
    a sentence whose subject was removed after it was written.
  endcomment
  if product_title != blank
    assign decrease_label = 'products.product.quantity_decrease' | t: product: product_title
    assign increase_label = 'products.product.quantity_increase' | t: product: product_title
  else
    assign decrease_label = 'products.product.quantity_decrease_plain' | t
    assign increase_label = 'products.product.quantity_increase_plain' | t
  endif
-%}

<quantity-selector
  class="quantity-selector quantity-selector--{{ variant_class }} {{ class }}"
  data-min="{{ qty_min }}"
  data-step="{{ qty_step }}"
  {% if max %}data-max="{{ max }}"{% endif %}
  {{ attributes }}
>
  <label class="{{ label_class }}" for="{{ id }}">
    {{ 'products.product.quantity' | t }}
  </label>

  <div class="quantity-selector__control">
    <button
      type="button"
      class="quantity-selector__button"
      data-ref="decrease"
      aria-controls="{{ id }}"
      aria-label="{{ decrease_label | strip | escape }}"
    >
      {% render 'icon', icon: 'minus', size: glyph %}
    </button>

    <input
      id="{{ id }}"
      class="quantity-selector__input"
      data-ref="input"
      type="number"
      name="{{ qty_name }}"
      value="{{ qty_value }}"
      min="{{ qty_min }}"
      step="{{ qty_step }}"
      {% if max %}max="{{ max }}"{% endif %}
      inputmode="numeric"
      autocomplete="off"
      aria-live="polite"
      {% if form != blank %}form="{{ form }}"{% endif %}
    >

    <button
      type="button"
      class="quantity-selector__button"
      data-ref="increase"
      aria-controls="{{ id }}"
      aria-label="{{ increase_label | strip | escape }}"
    >
      {% render 'icon', icon: 'plus', size: glyph %}
    </button>
  </div>

  {%- comment -%}
    Quantity rules are a merchant setting the customer cannot discover by
    trying — a step of 6 looks like a broken plus button otherwise.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  {%- if rule != blank -%}
    <p class="quantity-selector__rule">{{ rule }}</p>
  {%- endif -%}
</quantity-selector>
